"""Gauge fields, QCD, holography and quantum gravity: AdS/CFT, gauge theory, QCD, quantum geometry, quantum gravity, loop quantum gravity, quantum spacetime, spin foam, spin network, string theory."""

NARRATIVES = {
    "ads_cft_correspondence": [
        ("p", r"A scalar field in anti-de Sitter space does not radiate away to infinity. Near the boundary its amplitude falls off as a definite power of a radial coordinate. The AdS/CFT correspondence identifies that power with the scaling dimension of an operator in a conformal field theory defined on the boundary. The relation follows from the bulk wave equation alone. In Poincaré coordinates the metric of $(d+1)$-dimensional anti-de Sitter space with curvature radius $L$ is"),
        ("eq", r"""ds^2=\frac{L^2}{z^2}\big(dz^2+\eta_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu\big),\qquad z>0,"""),
        ("p", r"where $x^\mu$ are the $d$ coordinates of the boundary, which is reached as $z\to0$. A free scalar of mass $m$ obeys $(\Box-m^2)\phi=0$, with $\Box\phi=g^{-1/2}\partial_a(g^{1/2}g^{ab}\partial_b\phi)$. Near the boundary the derivatives along $x$ carry an extra factor $z^2$ relative to the radial terms. A power law $\phi\propto z^\Delta$ is therefore tested against the radial part only. Using $\sqrt g=(L/z)^{d+1}$ and $g^{zz}=z^2/L^2$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\Box\,z^\Delta=\frac{z^{d+1}}{L^2}\,\partial_z\big(z^{1-d}\partial_z z^\Delta\big)
=\frac{\Delta(\Delta-d)}{L^2}\,z^\Delta
\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad \Delta(\Delta-d)=m^2L^2."""),
        ("p", r"The two roots are $\Delta_\pm=d/2\pm\sqrt{d^2/4+m^2L^2}$, and their sum is $d$. A general solution behaves as $\phi\to z^{d-\Delta}\phi_0(x)+z^{\Delta}A(x)$ with $\Delta=\Delta_+$. The coefficient $\phi_0$ of the slower falloff is fixed by a boundary condition. The rescaling $z\to\lambda z$, $x\to\lambda x$ is an isometry of the metric, and under it $\phi_0$ transforms as a field of dimension $d-\Delta$. The correspondence treats $\phi_0$ as a source $J$ coupled to a boundary operator $\mathcal O$. The coupling $\int d^dx\,J\mathcal O$ is dimensionless only if $\mathcal O$ has dimension $\Delta$. The dictionary is an equality of generating functionals:"),
        ("eq", r"""Z_{\rm CFT}[J]=\Big\langle \exp\int d^dx\,J\,\mathcal O\Big\rangle_{\rm CFT}
=Z_{\rm bulk}\big[\phi\to z^{d-\Delta}J\big]."""),
        ("p", r"Both roots are real only if $m^2L^2\geq-d^2/4$. This is the Breitenlohner--Freedman bound. A field with slightly negative mass squared is stable in anti-de Sitter space, because the curvature confines it and its gradient energy outweighs the negative mass term. In the conformal field theory $\Delta$ fixes the power law of the two-point function, $\langle\mathcal O(x)\mathcal O(0)\rangle\propto|x|^{-2\Delta}$. The bulk calculation reproduces this from the propagation of $\phi$ between two boundary points."),
        ("p", r"The best-tested case pairs string theory on $AdS_5\times S^5$ with $\mathcal N=4$ supersymmetric $SU(N)$ gauge theory, so $d=4$. The dilaton is massless in five dimensions, so $\Delta=4$. It couples to the Lagrangian density $\operatorname{Tr}F^2$ of the gauge theory, whose dimension is four. Kaluza--Klein modes on the five-sphere give scalars with $m^2L^2=k(k-4)$ for integers $k\geq2$. The relation gives $\Delta=2+|k-2|=k$, which matches symmetrized traces of $k$ scalar fields of the gauge theory. The $k=2$ mode sits exactly at the bound, $m^2L^2=-4$, and is stable. Supersymmetry protects these dimensions, so the free-field value can be compared with the classical bulk value."),
        ("p", r"The classical bulk calculation requires $L$ to be large compared with the string length and the Planck length. For the $\mathcal N=4$ theory this means large $N$ and large 't Hooft coupling $\lambda=g_{\rm YM}^2N$, since $L^4/\alpha'^2=\lambda$. Away from that regime string and quantum corrections enter. The correspondence remains a conjecture: it has passed many quantitative checks of protected spectra and correlators, but it has no general proof. Because the bulk contains gravity, it is used to study quantum gravity in anti-de Sitter space through an ordinary gauge theory."),
    ],
    "gauge_theory": [
        ("p", r"An electron beam split around a long, shielded solenoid recombines with its interference fringes displaced. The electrons travel only where the magnetic field vanishes. The displacement follows from requiring that the phase of a wave function can be chosen independently at every point. Let $\psi(\mathbf r,t)$ be the wave function of a particle of charge $q$. Consider the local change $\psi\to e^{iq\chi/\hbar}\psi$, with $\chi(\mathbf r,t)$ an arbitrary real function."),
        ("p", r"The probability density is unchanged, but the gradient acquires an extra term: $\nabla(e^{iq\chi/\hbar}\psi)=e^{iq\chi/\hbar}(\nabla\psi+iq\psi\nabla\chi/\hbar)$. The Schrödinger equation with the ordinary gradient therefore changes form. The extra term is cancelled by a vector potential that shifts as $\mathbf A\to\mathbf A+\nabla\chi$, entering through the covariant derivative"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathbf D=\nabla-\frac{iq}{\hbar}\mathbf A,\qquad
\mathbf D'\big(e^{iq\chi/\hbar}\psi\big)=e^{iq\chi/\hbar}\,\mathbf D\psi,\\
[D_x,D_y]=-\frac{iq}{\hbar}\big(\partial_xA_y-\partial_yA_x\big)=-\frac{iq}{\hbar}B_z."""),
        ("p", r"The same argument for the time derivative requires a scalar potential with $\varphi\to\varphi-\partial_t\chi$. The Hamiltonian becomes $(\mathbf p-q\mathbf A)^2/2m+q\varphi$, the minimal coupling of electrodynamics. The commutator of covariant derivatives is the magnetic field. It is the part of the potential that no choice of $\chi$ can remove. Carrying the phase around a closed path $\gamma$ and applying Stokes' theorem gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\delta_{\rm AB}=\frac{q}{\hbar}\oint_\gamma\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf l
=\frac{q}{\hbar}\int_\Sigma\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S=\frac{q\Phi}{\hbar},"""),
        ("p", r"where $\Sigma$ is a surface bounded by $\gamma$ and $\Phi$ the enclosed flux. Outside an ideal solenoid the potential $A_\theta=\Phi/2\pi r$ has zero curl, so the field vanishes on both paths. Locally $\mathbf A=\nabla(\Phi\theta/2\pi)$, but the angle $\theta$ is not single valued around the solenoid. The potential therefore cannot be removed everywhere. The beams passing on either side differ in phase by $q\Phi/\hbar$, and only this phase modulo $2\pi$ is observable. For electrons the fringes move by one period when $\Phi$ changes by $h/e=4.14\times10^{-15}$ Wb."),
        ("p", r"Tonomura and colleagues tested this in 1986 with a toroidal magnet coated by a superconducting layer. The layer kept the electron wave out of the field region and quantized the trapped flux in units of $h/2e$. The measured phase shift was $0$ or $\pi$, as the relation predicts for an even or odd number of flux quanta."),
        ("p", r"When the internal state has several components, as for the three colours of a quark, the phase becomes a position-dependent unitary matrix $U(x)$. The potential becomes a matrix $A_\mu=A_\mu^aT^a$ built from the generators $T^a$ of the group, and the field strength acquires a commutator:"),
        ("eq", r"""D_\mu=\partial_\mu-igA_\mu,\qquad
F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu-ig[A_\mu,A_\nu],\\
F_{\mu\nu}\to UF_{\mu\nu}U^{-1},\qquad
W(\gamma)=\operatorname{Tr}\mathcal P\exp\Big(ig\oint_\gamma A_\mu dx^\mu\Big)."""),
        ("p", r"Here $g$ is the coupling and $\mathcal P$ orders the matrices along the path. The field strength is no longer invariant; it rotates in the internal space. Observables are traces, such as $\operatorname{Tr}F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}$ and the Wilson loop $W(\gamma)$ of a test charge carried around $\gamma$. In the Hamiltonian description Gauss's law generates the time-independent gauge transformations, and physical states are those it annihilates. The commutator term makes the gauge field respond to its own charge, so gluons interact with one another; quantum chromodynamics develops this case. The Aharonov--Bohm phase is the Abelian Wilson loop, and the same loop variables underlie the spin networks of loop quantum gravity."),
    ],
    "quantum_chromodynamics": [
        ("p", r"Electrons scattered from protons at large momentum transfer behave as if they struck nearly free point-like quarks. At low momentum transfer the same quarks are never observed in isolation. Both facts follow from the dependence of the strong coupling on the resolution scale. Quantum chromodynamics is an $SU(3)$ gauge theory. Each quark field $q_f$ of flavour $f$ and mass $m_f$ has three colour components. Eight gluon fields $A^a_\mu$ act on them through generators $T^a$ with $[T^a,T^b]=if^{abc}T^c$ and $\operatorname{Tr}T^aT^b=\delta^{ab}/2$. In natural units $\hbar=c=1$,"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathcal L=-\frac12\operatorname{Tr}F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
+\sum_f\bar q_f\big(i\gamma^\mu D_\mu-m_f\big)q_f,\\
D_\mu=\partial_\mu-ig_sA^a_\mu T^a,\qquad
F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu-ig_s[A_\mu,A_\nu]."""),
        ("p", r"Squaring the commutator term gives three-gluon and four-gluon vertices, so gluons carry colour themselves. Virtual quarks and gluons make the effective coupling depend on the momentum scale $\mu$ at which it is measured. At one loop,"),
        ("eq", r"""\mu\frac{dg_s}{d\mu}=-\frac{\beta_0}{16\pi^2}g_s^3,\qquad
\beta_0=\frac{11}{3}C_A-\frac43T_Fn_f=11-\frac23n_f,"""),
        ("p", r"with $C_A=3$ the quadratic Casimir of the gluon representation, $T_F=1/2$ the normalization of the quark generators, and $n_f$ the number of quark flavours light compared with $\mu$. The two signs have a physical origin. A quark loop polarizes the vacuum as an electron loop does in electrodynamics: virtual pairs screen a test charge. In a background colour-magnetic field, a massless field of spin $s$ contributes in proportion to $(-1)^{2s}[(2s)^2-1/3]$. The term $-1/3$ is the diamagnetic response of orbital motion, and $(2s)^2$ is the paramagnetic response of the spin. The factor $(-1)^{2s}$ is the sign of a fermion loop."),
        ("p", r"For gluons, $s=1$ gives $4-1/3=11/3$, and the spin term dominates. A Dirac quark gives $-2/3$ per flavour. The vacuum then acts as a medium with magnetic permeability above one. Relativistic invariance makes its dielectric constant the inverse, below one, so a colour charge is antiscreened. In terms of $\alpha_s=g_s^2/4\pi$ the equation integrates to"),
        ("eq", r"""\alpha_s(Q^2)=\frac{\alpha_s(\mu^2)}{1+\frac{\beta_0}{4\pi}\alpha_s(\mu^2)\ln(Q^2/\mu^2)}
=\frac{4\pi}{\beta_0\ln(Q^2/\Lambda^2)}."""),
        ("p", r"With five light flavours $\beta_0=23/3$. Starting from the measured $\alpha_s=0.118$ at the $Z$ mass, $91.2$ GeV, the one-loop formula gives $\alpha_s=0.173$ at 10 GeV and $0.088$ at 1 TeV. Determinations from tau decays, quarkonia, jet rates and the $Z$ width follow this decrease. The one-loop scale is $\Lambda\approx90$ MeV. Higher orders and the renormalization scheme change $\Lambda$, so its value is quoted with both specified. Asymptotic freedom requires $\beta_0>0$, that is at most sixteen flavours."),
        ("p", r"The formula holds while $\alpha_s$ is small. Its divergence as $Q$ approaches $\Lambda$ marks the breakdown of perturbation theory, not a measured infinity. Confinement and hadron masses are computed by lattice simulation. There the Wilson loop of a static quark--antiquark pair gives a potential that grows linearly with separation. The slope, the string tension, is close to 1 GeV per femtometre, until pair creation breaks the flux tube. A similar tension fixes the slope of meson Regge trajectories, which motivated the string models described under string theory. The underlying covariant derivative is derived under gauge theory."),
    ],
    "quantum_geometry": [
        ("p", r"A tetrahedron in flat space is fixed by its six edge lengths. Once its four face areas are known, two further numbers, such as two dihedral angles, specify its shape. Quantum geometry asks what happens when areas and angles become operators. For a single tetrahedron the answer is exact. The face areas can be sharp, but two angles cannot be sharp together when the volume differs from zero."),
        ("p", r"Describe face $i$ by its area vector $\mathbf A_i$, normal to the face with length equal to its area. For a tetrahedron the four outward vectors sum to zero. Take edge vectors $\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c$ from one vertex, so that three faces have $\mathbf A_1=\frac12\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$, $\mathbf A_2=\frac12\mathbf b\times\mathbf c$ and $\mathbf A_3=\frac12\mathbf c\times\mathbf a$. Vector algebra gives $\mathbf A_1\cdot(\mathbf A_2\times\mathbf A_3)=\frac18[\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)]^2$, and the volume is $V=|\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)|/6$. Hence"),
        ("eq", r"""\sum_{i=1}^4\mathbf A_i=0,\qquad
V^2=\frac29\,\big|\mathbf A_1\cdot(\mathbf A_2\times\mathbf A_3)\big|."""),
        ("p", r"In loop quantum gravity and in spin-foam models each area vector becomes an operator proportional to an angular momentum, $\mathbf A_i=8\pi\gamma l_P^2\,\mathbf J_i$. Here $l_P$ is the Planck length and $\gamma$ the Barbero--Immirzi parameter. Face $i$ carries a spin $j_i$, so its area $8\pi\gamma l_P^2\sqrt{j_i(j_i+1)}$ is sharp. The condition $\sum_i\mathbf J_i=0$ selects the rotation-invariant states of four spins. Their number is the number of ways to couple the four spins to total spin zero. The products $\mathbf J_1\cdot\mathbf J_2$ and $\mathbf J_1\cdot\mathbf J_3$ measure angles between face normals. With $[J^a,J^b]=i\epsilon^{abc}J^c$ and $\hbar=1$, their commutator is"),
        ("eq", r"""[\mathbf J_1\cdot\mathbf J_2,\,\mathbf J_1\cdot\mathbf J_3]=i\,\mathbf J_1\cdot(\mathbf J_2\times\mathbf J_3)\equiv iQ,\\
V=(8\pi\gamma l_P^2)^{3/2}\sqrt{\tfrac29\,|Q|}."""),
        ("p", r"Two angles commute only on states of zero volume. A quantum tetrahedron with volume therefore has an uncertainty relation between its dihedral angles, as a spin has between its components. Its classical shape exists only as a mean value. The relative spread of the angles decreases as the spins grow."),
        ("p", r"The smallest case has four faces of spin $1/2$. Two pairs of spin-$1/2$ faces couple to total spin zero in two independent ways, so the shape space is two-dimensional. Let faces 1 and 2 couple to spin $k\in\{0,1\}$. Then $\mathbf J_1\cdot\mathbf J_2=[k(k+1)-3/2]/2$ takes the values $-3/4$ and $1/4$. The operator $Q$ has eigenvalues $\pm\sqrt3/4$, one for each orientation. Both give the volume $V\approx0.31\,(8\pi\gamma)^{3/2}l_P^3$, about $4.5\,l_P^3$ for $\gamma=0.2375$. In either volume eigenstate $\mathbf J_1\cdot\mathbf J_2$ has mean $-1/4$ and variance $1/4$: the angle is maximally uncertain."),
        ("p", r"The numerical prefactor of the volume depends on how the operator is regularized on a graph. The vanishing of $Q$ at nodes with fewer than four faces does not. The calculation is kinematical: it concerns one tetrahedron at one instant, before any dynamics of general relativity is imposed. Gluing quantum tetrahedra gives the four-valent nodes of a spin network, and their histories are summed in spin foam models. Whether these discrete spectra describe physical space depends on that dynamics, which remains open, and on the value of $\gamma$ discussed under loop quantum gravity."),
    ],
    "quantum_gravity": [
        ("p", r"A black hole of one solar mass is predicted to emit thermal radiation at about sixty nanokelvin. That prediction comes from quantum fields on a classical curved spacetime. A theory in which the geometry itself is quantized does not yet exist in tested form. The scale at which it should matter follows from dimensional analysis. Planck's constant $\hbar$, Newton's constant $G$ and the speed of light $c$ form only one length, one time and one mass:"),
        ("eq", r"""l_P=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar G}{c^3}}=1.62\times10^{-35}\,{\rm m},\qquad
t_P=\frac{l_P}{c}=5.39\times10^{-44}\,{\rm s},\\
m_P=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar c}{G}}=2.18\times10^{-8}\,{\rm kg},\qquad
m_Pc^2=1.22\times10^{19}\,{\rm GeV}."""),
        ("p", r"Near the Planck mass the Compton wavelength $\hbar/mc$ of a particle equals its Schwarzschild radius $2Gm/c^2$, up to a factor of order one. A probe localized to the Planck length would therefore form a black hole around itself. Gravitational corrections to a scattering amplitude at energy $E$ have relative size $(E/m_Pc^2)^2$. At the 13 TeV of the Large Hadron Collider this ratio is about $10^{-30}$."),
        ("p", r"The Hawking temperature can be derived in a few steps. Outside a mass $M$ the Schwarzschild metric has $g_{tt}=-(1-r_s/r)c^2$ with $r_s=2GM/c^2$. In a thermal state the imaginary time $\tau=it$ is periodic with period $\hbar/k_BT$. This follows from the Gibbs operator $e^{-\beta H}$ of quantum statistical mechanics, which evolves states in imaginary time. Near the horizon set $r=r_s+\rho^2/4r_s$. The Euclidean metric becomes"),
        ("eq", r"""ds^2\simeq\Big(\frac{\rho}{2r_s}\Big)^2c^2d\tau^2+d\rho^2,\qquad
\frac{\hbar}{k_BT_H}=\frac{4\pi r_s}{c}
\;\Rightarrow\; T_H=\frac{\hbar c^3}{8\pi GMk_B}."""),
        ("p", r"This is a plane in polar coordinates with angle $c\tau/2r_s$. It is smooth at $\rho=0$ only if that angle has period $2\pi$, which fixes the period of $\tau$ and hence the temperature. Hawking obtained the same result from the mixing of positive and negative frequencies of a quantum field during collapse. For a solar mass $T_H=6.2\times10^{-8}$ K. This is far below the 2.7 K of the cosmic microwave background, so such holes absorb more radiation than they emit. Only holes lighter than about $4.5\times10^{22}$ kg, less than a lunar mass, are hotter than the background. The associated Bekenstein--Hawking entropy is $S=k_BA/4l_P^2$, with $A$ the horizon area, about $10^{77}k_B$ for a solar mass."),
        ("p", r"The calculation treats geometry as classical and matter fields as quantum. It is controlled when the horizon curvature is small on the Planck scale, that is for $M\gg m_P$. Other established results share this semiclassical status. Colella, Overhauser and Werner measured the gravitational phase shift of neutrons in an interferometer. Treating general relativity as an effective field theory gives calculable quantum corrections to the Newtonian potential at energies far below $m_Pc^2$, too small to measure."),
        ("p", r"A theory valid at the Planck scale itself is not established. Perturbative quantization of general relativity needs new counterterms at each loop order, so it loses predictive power there. Open questions include the final stage of evaporation and whether the radiation carries the information of what fell in. The microscopic origin of the entropy is also open in general. String theory, loop quantum gravity and the AdS/CFT correspondence give partial answers in restricted settings. None of them has been tested by experiment."),
    ],
    "loop_quantum_gravity": [
        ("p", r"If space is quantized, the area of a surface should take discrete values, as the energy of an atom does. Loop quantum gravity gives a definite spectrum for area. It quantizes general relativity written with an $SU(2)$ connection $A^i_a$ and its conjugate momentum $E^a_i$, a densitized triad that encodes the spatial metric. In units with $c=1$, the Poisson bracket and the area of a surface $S$ are"),
        ("eq", r"""\{A^i_a(x),E^b_j(y)\}=8\pi G\gamma\,\delta^b_a\delta^i_j\,\delta^3(x-y),\\
A(S)=\int_Sd^2\sigma\,\sqrt{n_aE^a_i\,n_bE^b_i}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $\gamma$ is the Barbero--Immirzi parameter, $a$ a spatial index, $i$ an internal index and $n_a$ the normal to $S$. The states are functions of holonomies, the path-ordered exponentials of $A$ along the edges of a graph $\Gamma$. Each edge carries a spin $j$. Quantization turns $E$ into $-i\,8\pi\gamma l_P^2\,\delta/\delta A$, with $l_P^2=\hbar G$. On an edge crossing $S$, the flux of $E$ through $S$ acts as $8\pi\gamma l_P^2$ times the $SU(2)$ generator $\mathbf J$ of that edge. The square root of $\mathbf J^2=j(j+1)$ then gives a finite contribution from each crossing:"),
        ("eq", r"""\hat A(S)\,|\Gamma,\{j\}\rangle=8\pi\gamma l_P^2\sum_{p\in S\cap\Gamma}\sqrt{j_p(j_p+1)}\;|\Gamma,\{j\}\rangle."""),
        ("p", r"The sum runs over the points where edges puncture the surface. A surface crossed by no edge has zero area. The spectrum is discrete, and its smallest nonzero eigenvalue comes from one spin-$1/2$ puncture: $a_0=4\sqrt3\pi\gamma l_P^2$. The formula holds for punctures by edges; nodes lying on the surface add further terms. The result is kinematical. The operator is defined before the Hamiltonian constraint is solved, and whether its spectrum survives in the full dynamics is debated."),
        ("p", r"The parameter $\gamma$ is the main ambiguity. Classically it labels a family of canonical variables, $A=\Gamma+\gamma K$, built from the spin connection $\Gamma$ and the extrinsic curvature $K$. Every value describes the same general relativity. After quantization the area spectrum scales with $\gamma$, and different values give inequivalent quantum theories. The classical theory does not fix it."),
        ("p", r"The usual way to fix $\gamma$ is to count horizon states. A black-hole horizon of area $A$ is punctured by many edges. Counting the spin configurations compatible with that area gives an entropy proportional to $A/\gamma$. Requiring the Bekenstein--Hawking value $S=k_BA/4l_P^2$ yields an equation for $\gamma$. In the counting of Domagala and Lewandowski, and of Meissner, it reads"),
        ("eq", r"""1=\sum_{j=1/2,\,1,\,3/2,\ldots}2\,e^{-2\pi\gamma\sqrt{j(j+1)}}
\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad \gamma=0.2375\ldots,\qquad a_0\approx5.2\,l_P^2."""),
        ("p", r"With this value the smallest area is about $1.4\times10^{-69}$ m$^2$. An earlier count kept only spin-$1/2$ punctures and gave $\gamma=\ln2/(\pi\sqrt3)\approx0.127$. Other counting rules give other values, and the logarithmic correction to the entropy also depends on the rule. The procedure therefore matches one parameter; it does not predict the entropy. No experiment has resolved areas at this scale. Within the theory, the discreteness of area and volume for spin-network states is established, as described under spin network. The dynamics is formulated through spin foams and remains open."),
    ],
    "quantum_spacetime": [
        ("p", r"To locate an event within a distance $\Delta x$, a probe must carry momentum of order $\hbar/\Delta x$. Its energy also curves spacetime. When $\Delta x$ approaches the Planck length, the probe would collapse into a black hole larger than the region probed. Heuristic arguments of this kind, together with estimates from string scattering, suggest a smallest resolvable length. Quantum spacetime models encode it by deforming commutators of positions and momenta. None has been derived from a complete theory or confirmed by experiment. They are proposals whose consequences can be calculated and bounded."),
        ("p", r"A one-dimensional model of Kempf, Mangano and Mann replaces the canonical commutator by a momentum-dependent one. The Robertson relation $\Delta x\,\Delta p\geq|\langle[x,p]\rangle|/2$, with $\langle p^2\rangle=(\Delta p)^2+\langle p\rangle^2$, then gives"),
        ("eq", r"""[x,p]=i\hbar\big(1+\beta p^2\big),\qquad
\Delta x\,\Delta p\geq\frac\hbar2\Big(1+\beta(\Delta p)^2+\beta\langle p\rangle^2\Big),"""),
        ("p", r"where $\beta>0$ has dimensions of inverse momentum squared. For $\langle p\rangle=0$, dividing by $\Delta p$ shows that the bound on $\Delta x$ is smallest at $\Delta p=1/\sqrt\beta$. No state can have a smaller position uncertainty than"),
        ("eq", r"""\Delta x_{\min}=\hbar\sqrt\beta,\qquad
\beta=\frac{\beta_0}{(m_Pc)^2}\;\Rightarrow\;\Delta x_{\min}=\sqrt{\beta_0}\,l_P,"""),
        ("p", r"with $\beta_0$ dimensionless and $m_P$ the Planck mass. Position eigenstates no longer exist; the most localized states are packets of width $\Delta x_{\min}$. Momentum can still be sharp, and a momentum-space wave function describes the state. As an effective description the model is meant for momenta well below $m_Pc/\sqrt{\beta_0}$, where the deformation is small."),
        ("p", r"A harmonic oscillator of mass $m$ and frequency $\omega$ shows a concrete consequence. In the variable $u=\arctan(\sqrt\beta\,p)$ the position acts as $i\hbar\sqrt\beta\,\partial_u$. The eigenvalue problem becomes a Pöschl--Teller equation with the exact spectrum"),
        ("eq", r"""E_n=\hbar\omega\Big[\big(n+\tfrac12\big)\sqrt{1+\tfrac{\kappa^2}{4}}+\frac\kappa2\big(n^2+n+\tfrac12\big)\Big],
\qquad \kappa=\beta m\hbar\omega."""),
        ("p", r"For small $\kappa$ each level shifts by $\beta m\hbar^2\omega^2(2n^2+2n+1)/4$, and the spacing grows linearly with $n$. The size is the issue. For an electron with $\hbar\omega=1$ meV and $\beta_0=1$, $\kappa\approx3\times10^{-54}$. Experiments therefore bound $\beta_0$ rather than measure it, and the bounds lie many orders of magnitude above one. How the deformation applies to composite bodies is unresolved, which complicates tests with massive oscillators."),
        ("p", r"Other proposals act on spacetime coordinates directly. Snyder in 1947 made coordinates fail to commute, with commutators proportional to the Lorentz generators and to a length squared. Lorentz symmetry is kept, and the spatial coordinates acquire discrete spectra. Doplicher, Fredenhagen and Roberts derived spacetime uncertainty relations such as $\Delta x^0\sum_i\Delta x^i\gtrsim l_P^2$. Their condition is that localizing an event must not create a horizon. Loop quantum gravity instead quantizes areas and volumes of space, and string theory limits resolution through the size of the string. The scales involved are set out under quantum gravity."),
    ],
    "spin_foam": [
        ("p", r"The amplitude for a particle to travel between two points is a sum over the paths joining them. Spin foam models apply the same rule to the geometry of space, described at each instant by a spin network. The history of a spin network is a two-dimensional complex whose faces carry spins and whose edges carry rotation-invariant couplings of those spins. In three spacetime dimensions the construction can be checked against classical gravity through the Ponzano--Regge model. Spacetime is triangulated by tetrahedra, and each edge $e$ is labelled by a spin $j_e$. The spin is read as a length $l_e=j_e+1/2$ in units of the three-dimensional Planck length. Up to sign conventions the amplitude is"),
        ("eq", r"""Z=\sum_{\{j_e\}}\ \prod_e(2j_e+1)\ \prod_t\begin{Bmatrix}j_1&j_2&j_3\\ j_4&j_5&j_6\end{Bmatrix}_t ."""),
        ("p", r"Here the bracket is the Wigner $6j$ symbol of the six spins on tetrahedron $t$. It is the recoupling coefficient of three angular momenta known from atomic spectroscopy. The factor $2j_e+1$ is the dimension of the spin-$j_e$ representation. The $6j$ symbol vanishes unless each triangle obeys the triangle inequality with an integer sum of spins. Only spins that can form real triangles contribute."),
        ("p", r"The connection with gravity appears at large spins. Ponzano and Regge found the following asymptotic form, and Roberts later proved it:"),
        ("eq", r"""\begin{Bmatrix}j_1&j_2&j_3\\ j_4&j_5&j_6\end{Bmatrix}\simeq
\frac{1}{\sqrt{12\pi V}}\cos\Big(\sum_{e=1}^6 l_e\theta_e+\frac\pi4\Big),\qquad l_e=j_e+\tfrac12."""),
        ("p", r"Here $V$ is the volume of the Euclidean tetrahedron with edge lengths $l_e$. The angle $\theta_e$ is the exterior dihedral angle at edge $e$, between the outward normals of the two faces that share it. The sum $\sum_el_e\theta_e$ is the Regge action of the tetrahedron. Regge calculus is the discrete form of general relativity, with curvature concentrated on edges as deficit angles. The amplitude is therefore a sum of $e^{iS}$ and $e^{-iS}$ for the two orientations. This is the form expected of a gravitational path integral in the semiclassical limit."),
        ("p", r"For a regular tetrahedron with all spins equal to $N$, the volume is $V=l^3/6\sqrt2$ with $l=N+1/2$. Every exterior angle is $\pi-\arccos(1/3)\approx1.911$. At $N=20$ the exact $6j$ symbol is $-5.029\times10^{-3}$, and the asymptotic formula gives $-5.034\times10^{-3}$. At $N=40$ the two agree to three parts in $10^4$. The sign changes with $N$ follow the phase $6l\theta$ of the cosine."),
        ("p", r"In three dimensions gravity has no local propagating degrees of freedom, and the Ponzano--Regge model is a quantization of it. The sum over spins diverges for triangulations with interior vertices. The divergence reflects a residual symmetry and is removed by gauge fixing. With a positive cosmological constant the model is replaced by the finite Turaev--Viro state sum. In four dimensions, models such as that of Engle, Pereira, Rovelli and Livine assign amplitudes to four-simplices. Their large-spin limit again contains the Regge action. Whether sums over many simplices reproduce general relativity, with its gravitational waves, is not established. The boundary states of a spin foam are the spin networks of loop quantum gravity, and each tetrahedron is the quantum tetrahedron described under quantum geometry."),
    ],
    "spin_network": [
        ("p", r"In a lattice gauge theory, a line of electric flux gives a definite flux through every surface it pierces and none through surfaces it misses. Loop quantum gravity gives space the same structure. Area belongs to lines that pierce a surface, and volume to points where several lines meet. The states with this property are spin networks, built like lattice gauge fields but with the group $SU(2)$ on an arbitrary graph. Each edge $e$ of a graph $\Gamma$ carries a spin $j_e$. Each node $n$ carries an intertwiner $\iota_n$, a tensor that couples the spins meeting there to total spin zero. The state depends on the connection $A$ through the holonomies $h_e$ along the edges:"),
        ("eq", r"""\Psi_{\Gamma,j,\iota}[A]=\Big(\bigotimes_n\iota_n\Big)\cdot\Big(\bigotimes_e D^{(j_e)}\big(h_e[A]\big)\Big),\qquad
h_e=\mathcal P\exp\int_eA."""),
        ("p", r"Here $D^{(j)}$ is the spin-$j$ matrix of a group element, and the dot contracts each matrix index with an index of an intertwiner. Gauge transformations act at the nodes and rotate the indices of all edges meeting there. The state is invariant because each intertwiner is a rotation-invariant tensor. Whether such a tensor exists, and how many independent ones exist, follows from the addition of angular momenta."),
        ("p", r"The simplest graph is a closed loop with one two-valent node. Two spins couple to total spin zero only if they are equal, and then in exactly one way. The intertwiner is the identity map between the two edges, so the node can be removed. The state is the Wilson loop $\operatorname{Tr}D^{(j)}(h_\gamma)$ of the spin-$j$ representation around the loop $\gamma$. An edge of spin $1/2$ cannot join an edge of spin $1$ at a two-valent node: no invariant exists. The spin along a line therefore changes only where a third edge meets it. At a three-valent node the spins must satisfy $|j_1-j_2|\leq j_3\leq j_1+j_2$ with an integer sum. The intertwiner is again unique, a Clebsch--Gordan coefficient. Four spin-$1/2$ edges admit two independent intertwiners."),
        ("p", r"Geometric operators act on these states locally. For a surface $S$ crossed by edges at points $p$, with no node on the surface, the area is diagonal. The volume of a region $R$ is a sum of contributions from the nodes inside it:"),
        ("eq", r"""\hat A(S)\,\Psi=8\pi\gamma l_P^2\sum_{p\in S\cap\Gamma}\sqrt{j_p(j_p+1)}\;\Psi,\qquad
\hat V(R)\,\Psi=\sum_{n\in R}\hat V_n\Psi."""),
        ("p", r"Here $\gamma$ is the Barbero--Immirzi parameter and $l_P$ the Planck length. Each crossing contributes the square root of the Casimir operator of its edge. Consider a loop of spin $j$ that crosses a closed surface twice. The area eigenvalue is $16\pi\gamma l_P^2\sqrt{j(j+1)}$. For $j=1/2$ and $\gamma=0.2375$ this is $8\sqrt3\pi\gamma l_P^2\approx10.3\,l_P^2$. The volume of the loop is zero. A node contributes volume only if at least four edges meet there with tangents not all in one plane. For three edges, the triple product of their generators vanishes on the unique invariant state."),
        ("p", r"The loop therefore carries area but no volume. It is a flux line of geometry, in the same way that a Wilson line in lattice gauge theory is a line of electric flux. The triad measured by the area plays the part of the electric field, and its square gives the Casimir. These statements are kinematical. They hold on the state space before the Hamiltonian constraint is imposed. Diffeomorphism invariance makes only the combinatorics and knotting of the graph physical, not its coordinates. The four-valent node is worked out as the quantum tetrahedron under quantum geometry. The area spectrum and the value of $\gamma$ are discussed under loop quantum gravity."),
    ],
    "string_theory": [
        ("p", r"Mesons of increasing spin $J$ lie close to straight lines when $J$ is plotted against mass squared. A rotating relativistic string with its ends moving at the speed of light gives this relation. String theory begins from the quantum mechanics of such a string, and its best-established results concern the spectrum. A string of tension $T$ sweeps out a worldsheet $X^\mu(\tau,\sigma)$ in $D$-dimensional spacetime. With $\hbar=c=1$, the Nambu--Goto action is proportional to the area of that worldsheet:"),
        ("eq", r"""S=-T\int d\tau\,d\sigma\,\sqrt{-\det\big(\partial_\alpha X^\mu\,\partial_\beta X_\mu\big)},\qquad
T=\frac{1}{2\pi\alpha'}."""),
        ("p", r"Consider a straight open string of length $2R$ rotating rigidly, with free ends moving at the speed of light. Integrating the relativistic energy and angular momentum densities gives $E=\pi TR$ and $J=\pi TR^2/2$, so $J=\alpha'E^2$. The mesons $\rho(775)$, $a_2(1320)$ and $\rho_3(1690)$ fit $J\approx0.46+0.89M^2$, with $M$ in GeV. The slope corresponds to a tension of about $0.9$ GeV per femtometre, close to the flux-tube tension of quantum chromodynamics."),
        ("p", r"Quantization is simplest in light-cone gauge. It removes the two longitudinal directions and leaves $D-2$ transverse oscillators $\alpha^i_n$ for each harmonic $n$. For an open string with free ends the mass is"),
        ("eq", r"""\alpha'M^2=N+a,\qquad
N=\sum_{n\geq1}\sum_{i=1}^{D-2}\alpha^i_{-n}\alpha^i_n,\qquad
a=\frac{D-2}{2}\sum_{n\geq1}n\ \to\ -\frac{D-2}{24}."""),
        ("p", r"The constant $a$ is the zero-point energy of the oscillators, half a quantum per mode, summed over harmonics. With a cutoff, $\sum_nne^{-\epsilon n}=\epsilon^{-2}-1/12+O(\epsilon^2)$. The divergent term is removed by a local counterterm on the worldsheet. The finite remainder $-1/12$ equals the value $\zeta(-1)$ of the Riemann zeta function. The same subtraction gives the Casimir energy of a field between two plates."),
        ("p", r"The lowest excited level, $N=1$, consists of the states $\alpha^i_{-1}|0;k\rangle$, with one vector index taking $D-2$ values. A massive vector in $D$ dimensions has $D-1$ polarizations; only a massless one has $D-2$. Lorentz invariance, which light-cone gauge does not make manifest, therefore requires $1+a=0$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac{D-2}{24}=1\;\Rightarrow\;D=26,\qquad
\alpha'M^2=-1\ \ (N=0),\qquad \alpha'M^2=0\ \ (N=1)."""),
        ("p", r"A complete check confirms this: the Lorentz generators built from the oscillators close only for $D=26$ and $a=-1$. At level $N=2$ the $324$ states form a symmetric traceless tensor of $SO(25)$, the rotation group of a massive particle at rest. The ground state is a tachyon, which signals that the bosonic string in flat space is unstable."),
        ("p", r"Established results are of this kind: consistency conditions and spectra of a perturbative quantum theory. Closed strings contain a massless spin-two state whose low-energy interactions are those of general relativity. That is why the theory is studied as a theory of quantum gravity. With worldsheet fermions the critical dimension becomes ten, and a projection of the spectrum removes the tachyon. For certain supersymmetric black holes, counting string states reproduces the Bekenstein--Hawking entropy. No string excitation or extra dimension has been detected, and the string length is unknown. The choice among many four-dimensional solutions is open. The AdS/CFT correspondence supplies a nonperturbative formulation in anti-de Sitter backgrounds."),
    ],
}

